{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NgfEMeuqbSUkZK5zCeYKdX","type":"tool_result","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"review_brief.py — an attended launcher's review and critique briefs, rendered\nfrom the kit's own templates, and its review filed as a kit round file.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS (WI-852; the WI-841 retrospective, proposal §4(a), §6, §7.4).\nAn attended launcher — a coordinator working from an interactive session —\nreviewed its lanes from briefs it hand-wrote outside the repository. Two costs\nfollowed. The hand brief never carried the kit reviewer brief's two clauses\n(name the failure classes the change admits and hunt those first; say for a\nguard-adding remedy why the defect cannot be made unrepresentable), so findings\narrived as instances and were fixed as instances. And its review files used\ntheir own format, so the generated verdict rollup saw no such lane at all.\n\nSo this module RENDERS, it never launches (the launcher is another component's):\n\n  * `render_review` fills `prompts/reviewer.template.md` through `prompts.fill`\n    — strict both ways, so an unfilled slot refuses — with the lane's facts as\n    its values. The `{round_facts}` slot carries what only the launcher knows:\n    the range and whether the round is NARROW (the commits since the last\n    reviewed one, answering a named round file's findings) or FULL-LANE (claim\n    base to tip, the fresh review a landing is judged on), how to run the tests\n    in its scratch area, and that the launcher, not the reviewer, records the\n    verdict. The unattended loop fills that slot empty.\n  * `render_critique` fills `prompts/critique.template.md` over a work item's\n    spec, with a rubric the caller names as its rubric input — the scope\n    critique where a row is born.\n  * `file_review` writes a review's text, once it validates, as the round file\n    `docs/reviews/<lane>/NNN-REVIEW-X-<sha7>[-narrow].md` that\n    `kitlib.verdict.round_file` parses and `gen_verdict_rollup.py` renders. A\n    narrow round's name carries `-narrow`, so it never reads as the full-lane\n    review a landing needs.\n\nTHE BRIEF ASKS FOR THE KIT'S VERDICT FORMAT, AND ONE STEP FILES IT. A reviewer\nthe launcher keeps out of the worktree writes its verdict to a path in the\nlauncher's scratch area; `file` then validates it and places it. Validation is\nat that one boundary because the text is a model's output: it must open with\n`Reviewed: <the full sha>` (the binding between a review and the commit it\njudged), carry exactly one `VERDICT:` line whose `findings=N` equals its finding\nlines, and a CHANGES-REQUESTED must name a finding. Anything else is refused and\nnothing is written.\n\nA JUDGE'S BRIEF NEVER CARRIES THE JUDGED PARTY'S SELF-ASSESSMENT (prompts/\nREADME.md rule 3). There is no slot for a builder's report. A narrow round\nnames the earlier round file whose findings the range answers — another\nreviewer's judgement, labelled a claim under judgement, never the premise.\n\nStdlib only, Python 3.11+, Windows/POSIX.\n\nContracts: IF-288, IF-289 — the seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows\nof record in docs/requirements/interfaces.toml).\n\nContract IF-288: the attended launcher's command line, its arguments.\n    `review --wi WI-N --base REV --sha REV --scope narrow|full --tests T...\n    --scratch DIR --out FILE [--findings ROUND] [--rubric RUBRIC] [--python\n    EXE] [--root DIR]` writes one review round's brief to FILE: the
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
op_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3926,"cache_read_input_tokens":299689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3926},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8cf33a7-067d-4b49-9e30-523229883c86","uuid":"94fba157-1085-4b11-ac0c-9fee89b1f48c","timestamp":"2026-10-09T01:55:19.922Z","request_id":"req_011Cfqq6sNBgkvUpduRG73cr","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01T45u1zT32CDU438PgMxVSr":{"command":"cd /c/Projects/ai-template.wt/wi-852 && f=$(ls docs/work/active/wi-852/*.md) && cat >> \"$f\" <<'EOF'\n\n## Dispositions\n\nDrafted by the independent adjudicator in docs/reviews/wi-852-coordinator-renders-kit-briefs/001-ADJUDICATE-d75fdc2.md (amendment: SR-146 MEANING and blessed, SR-154 MEANING and not blessed; first-approval: LLR-313 and TC-333 returned).\n\n```toml\ntitle = \"Give SR-154's attended-recording clause a home in its requirement, and verify LLR-313's git and input refusals\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-146\", \"SR-154\"]\npriority = 5\n```\n\nThe build is not in question. Row text and tests only:\n\n1. SR-154: the amendment added an acceptance clause (an attended independent review verdict is recorded in the loop's round-record form, and a malformed or commit-unbound verdict is refused with no record), but the Requirement still covers only unattended work reaching integration. Do one of two things. Either amend the Requirement to state the attended recording obligation (who records, in what form, and when it is refused). Or move the clause to SR-146, which this change set already widened to attended renders, and restore SR-154's acceptance to its anchored text. Then bring the amended row back for re-attestation.\n2. Tests for LLR-313's unverified clauses, named in TC-333's Method, Expected and Evidence:\n   - `lane_commits` on a real git repository: a detached worktree (not on a branch), a revision that is not a commit, and a base that is not an ancestor each refuse, through the command line with exit 2 and no `--out` file written;\n   - a Round with an empty test list refuses;\n   - an empty named input (rubric or findings file) and a missing findings file refuse;\n   - a missing spec refuses the critique render too;\n   - one successful `review` and one successful `file` through `main`, each exiting 0, so IF-289's success code is driven.\n3. Bring LLR-313 and TC-333 back for first approval in the same sitting as SR-154's re-attestation. IF-288 and IF-289, which TC-333 claims to verify, will rest on the same evidence.\n\nObservation, not a ruling: `review_brief.py`'s docstring says a narrow round's `-narrow` suffix means it \"never reads as the full-lane review a landing needs\". But `kitlib.verdict.ROUND_FILE_RE` reads the suffix as an ordinary tag, and nothing in `score_reviews` or `integrate` distinguishes it. That is the lane reviewer's to check, not part of the rows above.\nEOF\npython -c \"\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,'project-trajectory/scripts'); import intake\nt=open(r'''$f''',encoding='utf-8').read(); d,r=intake.parse_dispositions(t,'WI-852'); print(r,[x['title'][:50] for x in d])\"\ngit add docs/reviews/wi-852-coordinator-renders-kit-briefs/001-ADJUDICATE-d75fdc2.md \"$f\" && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF' > /dev/null 2>&1; echo exit=$?; git log --oneline -1; git show --stat HEAD | tail -3\nreview: WI-852 adjudication at d75fdc2: amendment MEANING (SR-146 blessed, SR-154 not), first-approval RETURN (LLR-313, TC-333)\n\nCombined sitting (amendment;first-approval). SR-146 widens to attended\nrenders and adds the no-brief-on-refusal clause; it is blessed, and the\nclause is verified by the unfilled-slot command-line test. SR-154 adds an\nattended-recording acceptance clause that its unchanged Requirement\n(\"when unattended work reaches integration\") cannot carry, so it is not\nblessed. LLR-313 and TC-333 go back: lane_commits and IF-289's git\nrefusals are never driven (monkeypatched out), and the empty-test-list,\nempty-input and critique-missing-spec clauses have no verifying case.\nOne follow-up is drafted in the spec's ## Dispositions.\n\nWI: WI-852\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01T45u1zT32CDU438PgMxVSr":{"cwd":"/c/Projects/ai-template.wt/wi-852"}}}
